From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Wu Yuwei <yuweiwu@pm.me>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 13:08:54 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] Look line breaks up in a table

Finding line break opportunities with ICU4X took 1.53 ms of Speedometer's
long text (French). Under the default rules (one segmenter
configuration), analyze_text now looks them up in a table for characters
up to U+017F and from U+2000 to U+203F (line_table.rs: Latin-1, Latin
Extended-A and the commonest punctuation), by the class of the last
character before each one that isn't a space, whether spaces are
between them, and its class, as UAX #14's pair table does: 0.53 ms, with
no word given to ICU4X.

The table is what ICU4X's line segmenter gives, generated from it by a
test that fails when it is out of date and prints it anew. A word with a
character outside the table, or an entry that changes with the
characters around the pair (quotation marks beside spaces), is given to
ICU4X with the words on either side, and only its opportunities are
kept. The tests compare every text of up to four characters of the
classes and long random ones with ICU4X, and a debug build gives every
text looked up to ICU4X whole too and compares. Blink's
LazyLineBreakIterator looks pairs up to U+00AD in a table of its own.

AnalysisOptions::skip_word_boundaries (LayoutContext::set_word_boundaries,
on by default) leaves word boundaries out, a fifth of the analysis, for
text no one moves through by words with parley's clusters.
LayoutContext::line_breaks_looked_up counts the texts looked up and the
words given to ICU4X, for tests.
